/*!
    \page style-reference-containers.html
    \title Styling Containers and Windows
    \brief The style elements, states, and options of tab widgets, group boxes,
    splitters, toolboxes, toolbars, dock widgets, title bars, size grips, and
    rubber bands.

    This page is part of the \l{Widget Style Reference}. For an explanation
    of the element trees, screenshots, and tables, see that page. The common
    state flags and members that every widget sets are listed in
    \l{How a Style Draws a Widget#Common state flags and members}{How a Style
    Draws a Widget}.

    \section1 Tab widgets and tab bars

    \l QTabBar draws its tabs through the style. A tab bar is either part of a
    \l QTabWidget or standalone. A standalone bar draws its own base line. In a
    tab widget, the widget draws the frame around the pane and the bar sits on
    top of it.

    \l QTabWidget draws \c PE_FrameTabWidget with a
    \l QStyleOptionTabWidgetFrame. It asks the style where to put the tab bar,
    the pane, the contents, and the corner widgets with \c SE_TabWidgetTabPane,
    \c SE_TabWidgetTabContents, \c SE_TabWidgetTabBar,
    \c SE_TabWidgetLeftCorner, and \c SE_TabWidgetRightCorner, and it overlaps
    the bar and the pane by \c PM_TabBarBaseOverlap.

    \l QTabBar lays out the tabs itself, so the style has no control over tab
    placement. While laying out, the bar asks for \c CT_TabBarTab, which
    includes \c PM_TabBarTabHSpace and \c PM_TabBarTabVSpace, the extra width
    and height around the label. It then draws each tab with \c CE_TabBarTab and
    a \l QStyleOptionTab. The selected tab is drawn last so that a style can
    draw it over its neighbors.

    \list
    \li \c PE_FrameTabBarBase (\c PM_TabBarBaseHeight, \c PM_TabBarBaseOverlap),
        for a standalone bar, with a \l QStyleOptionTabBarBase
    \li \c CE_TabBarTab, once per tab
        \list
        \li \c CE_TabBarTabShape (\c PM_TabBarTabOverlap)
        \li \c CE_TabBarTabLabel (\c SE_TabBarTabText,
            \c SE_TabBarTabLeftButton, \c SE_TabBarTabRightButton,
            \c PM_TabBarTabShiftHorizontal, \c PM_TabBarTabShiftVertical,
            \c PM_TabBarIconSize)
        \endlist
    \li \c PE_IndicatorTabClose, drawn by the close button of a closable tab
        (\c SH_TabBar_CloseButtonPosition, \c PM_TabCloseIndicatorWidth,
        \c PM_TabCloseIndicatorHeight)
    \li \c PE_IndicatorTabTearLeft and \c PE_IndicatorTabTearRight, where tabs
        are cut off by the scroll buttons (\c SE_TabBarTearIndicatorLeft,
        \c SE_TabBarTearIndicatorRight)
    \endlist

    The scroll buttons that appear when the tabs don't fit are
    \l{QToolButton}{tool buttons}; the style places them with
    \c SE_TabBarScrollLeftButton and \c SE_TabBarScrollRightButton and sizes
    them with \c PM_TabBarScrollButtonWidth. The bar also consults
    \c SH_TabBar_Alignment, \c SH_TabBar_ElideMode, and
    \c SH_TabBar_PreferNoArrows.

    \image styles/tabwidget.webp
           {Tab widget with three tabs and a corner button, with the pane,
           contents, tab bar, corner, tab, and tab label rectangles outlined}

    \l QTabBar sets these state flags on each tab:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The tab is pressed.
        \row
            \li \c State_Selected
            \li The tab is the current tab.
        \row
            \li \c State_HasFocus
            \li The tab bar has focus and the tab is selected.
        \row
            \li \c State_MouseOver
            \li The mouse is over the tab.
    \endtable

    Individual tabs can be disabled even when the tab bar is enabled. The tab is
    active when the tab bar is active. The members of \l QStyleOptionTab are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li shape
            \li A \l QTabBar::Shape value: rounded or triangular tabs, and the
                side of the widget the bar is on.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The tab text.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The tab icon.
        \row
            \li iconSize
            \li The size of the icon.
        \row
            \li row
            \li The row the tab is in. Qt's tab bars have one row.
        \row
            \li position
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionTab::}{TabPosition} value: the tab's position
                relative to the other tabs, or \c Moving while the user drags
                it.
        \row
            \li selectedPosition
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionTab::}{SelectedPosition} value that tells
                whether the selected tab is next to this tab, and on which side.
        \row
            \li cornerWidgets
            \li Flags of the \l{QStyleOptionTab::}{CornerWidget} enum that tell
                which corner widgets the tab bar has.
        \row
            \li documentMode
            \li Whether the tab bar is in
                \l{QTabBar::documentMode}{document mode}.
        \row
            \li leftButtonSize, rightButtonSize
            \li The sizes of the buttons on the left and right side of the tab,
                such as the close button.
        \row
            \li features
            \li Flags of the \l{QStyleOptionTab::}{TabFeature} enum: whether the
                tab has a frame and whether it uses its minimum size hint.
        \row
            \li tabIndex
            \li The index of the tab in the bar.
    \endtable

    The members of \l QStyleOptionTabWidgetFrame are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li lineWidth
            \li The line width for drawing the panel.
        \row
            \li midLineWidth
            \li Always 0.
        \row
            \li shape
            \li The shape of the tabs on the tab bar.
        \row
            \li tabBarSize
            \li The size of the tab bar.
        \row
            \li tabBarRect
            \li The rectangle of the tab bar.
        \row
            \li selectedTabRect
            \li The rectangle of the selected tab, so that the frame can connect
                to it.
        \row
            \li leftCornerWidgetSize, rightCornerWidgetSize
            \li The sizes of the corner widgets, if any.
    \endtable

    \l QStyleOptionTabBarBase, used for \c PE_FrameTabBarBase, has the members
    \c shape, \c tabBarRect, \c selectedTabRect, and \c documentMode with the
    same meaning.

    \section1 Group boxes

    \l QGroupBox draws one complex control, \c CC_GroupBox, with a
    \l QStyleOptionGroupBox. It calculates its size hint from
    \c PM_IndicatorWidth, \c PM_IndicatorHeight, and \c PM_CheckBoxLabelSpacing.

    \list
    \li \c CC_GroupBox
        \list
        \li \c SC_GroupBoxFrame (\c PE_FrameGroupBox)
        \li \c SC_GroupBoxLabel, drawn with \l{QStyle::}{drawItemText()}, plus
            \c PE_FrameFocusRect when the box has focus
        \li \c SC_GroupBoxCheckBox (\c PE_IndicatorCheckBox), for a checkable
            box
        \li \c SC_GroupBoxContents, the area for the child widgets
        \endlist
    \endlist

    The style hints \c SH_GroupBox_TextLabelVerticalAlignment and
    \c SH_GroupBox_TextLabelColor control the title. Qt doesn't dictate how the
    checkbox is drawn; \l QCommonStyle uses \c PE_IndicatorCheckBox, so the
    element tree under
    \l{Styling Buttons and Input Widgets#Checkboxes and radio buttons}
    {Checkboxes and radio buttons} applies.

    \image styles/groupbox.webp
           {Checkable group box with two checkboxes inside, with the frame,
           checkbox, label, and contents subcontrols outlined}

    \l QGroupBox sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li The checkbox is checked.
        \row
            \li \c State_Off
            \li The checkbox is unchecked. A group box that isn't checkable sets
                neither \c State_On nor \c State_Off.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The checkbox or the title label is pressed.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionGroupBox are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li features
            \li \l QStyleOptionFrame::FrameFeatures flags that describe the
                frame, such as \c Flat for a flat group box.
        \row
            \li lineWidth
            \li The line width of the frame. Always 1.
        \row
            \li midLineWidth
            \li Always 0.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The title.
        \row
            \li textAlignment
            \li The alignment of the title.
        \row
            \li textColor
            \li The \c SH_GroupBox_TextLabelColor value. The group box only
                fills it in when the application hasn't set a \c WindowText
                brush in the palette; otherwise it stays invalid and
                \l QCommonStyle uses the palette instead.
    \endtable

    \section1 Splitters

    \l QSplitterHandle draws one control element, \c CE_Splitter, with a plain
    \l QStyleOption. The splitter sizes its handles with \c CT_Splitter and
    \c PM_SplitterWidth and asks for \c SH_Splitter_OpaqueResize to decide
    whether to resize the children while the user drags.

    \image styles/splitter.webp
           {Splitter with three text panes and the two handles outlined}

    The handle fills in the option itself rather than calling
    \l{QStyleOption::}{initFrom()}. It sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Horizontal
            \li The splitter is horizontal, so the handle is vertical.
        \row
            \li \c State_MouseOver
            \li The mouse is over the handle.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The handle is pressed.
        \row
            \li \c State_Enabled
            \li The handle is enabled.
    \endtable

    \section1 Toolboxes

    \l QToolBox keeps a collection of widgets and shows one at a time, with one
    tab button per widget in a vertical layout. Each button draws
    \c CE_ToolBoxTab with a \l QStyleOptionToolBox, and the buttons use
    \c PM_SmallIconSize for their icons.

    \list
    \li \c CE_ToolBoxTab, once per page
        \list
        \li \c CE_ToolBoxTabShape
        \li \c CE_ToolBoxTabLabel (\c SE_ToolBoxTabContents)
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \image styles/toolbox.webp
           {Toolbox with three tabs and the first page open, with the tabs and
           the current page outlined}

    The tab button sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Selected
            \li The tab is the current tab.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The tab is pressed.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionToolBox are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The icon on the tab.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The text on the tab.
        \row
            \li position
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionToolBox::}{TabPosition} value: the tab's
                position relative to the other tabs.
        \row
            \li selectedPosition
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionToolBox::}{SelectedPosition} value that tells
                whether the selected tab is next to this tab, and on which side.
    \endtable

    \section1 Toolbars

    Toolbars are part of the \l{QMainWindow}{main window framework}. A main
    window has four toolbar areas, one along each side, and each area can hold
    several lines of toolbars. \l QMainWindow positions the toolbars and fills
    in the position members of their style option.

    \l QToolBar draws \c CE_ToolBar with a \l QStyleOptionToolBar. When the
    toolbar floats in its own window, it also draws \c PE_FrameMenu around it. A
    movable toolbar draws its handle, and the separators between actions are
    small widgets that draw \c PE_IndicatorToolBarSeparator.

    \list
    \li \c CE_ToolBar (\c PM_ToolBarFrameWidth, \c PM_ToolBarItemMargin,
        \c PM_ToolBarItemSpacing)
        \list
        \li \c PE_PanelToolBar
        \endlist
    \li \c PE_FrameMenu, for a floating toolbar
    \li \c PE_IndicatorToolBarHandle (\c SE_ToolBarHandle,
        \c PM_ToolBarHandleExtent)
    \li \c PE_IndicatorToolBarSeparator (\c PM_ToolBarSeparatorExtent), drawn by
        each separator
    \endlist

    The tool buttons in the bar draw \c CC_ToolButton; see
    \l{Styling Buttons and Input Widgets#Tool buttons}{Tool buttons}. Their icon
    size defaults to \c PM_ToolBarIconSize. When the actions don't fit, the bar
    shows an extension button with the \c SP_ToolBarHorizontalExtensionButton or
    \c SP_ToolBarVerticalExtensionButton icon, sized by
    \c PM_ToolBarExtensionExtent.

    \image styles/toolbar.webp
           {Toolbar with a handle, three tool buttons, and a separator, with
           each element outlined}

    \l QToolBar sets this state flag:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Horizontal
            \li The toolbar is horizontal, that is, in the top or bottom toolbar
                area.
    \endtable

    The separators use a plain \l QStyleOption with \c State_Horizontal set when
    the toolbar is horizontal. The other members of \l QStyleOptionToolBar are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li features
            \li \l{QStyleOptionToolBar::}{ToolBarFeature} flags: \c Movable if
                the toolbar can be moved.
        \row
            \li lineWidth
            \li The width of the toolbar frame.
        \row
            \li midLineWidth
            \li Always 0.
        \row
            \li positionOfLine
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionToolBar::}{ToolBarPosition} value: the position
                of the toolbar's line within its toolbar area.
        \row
            \li positionWithinLine
            \li A \l{QStyleOptionToolBar::}{ToolBarPosition} value: the position
                of the toolbar within its line.
        \row
            \li toolBarArea
            \li The \l{Qt::ToolBarArea} the toolbar is in.
    \endtable

    \section1 Dock widgets

    \l QDockWidget draws its title bar with \c CE_DockWidgetTitle and a
    \l QStyleOptionDockWidget, unless the application installed a custom title
    bar widget or the dock widget floats with native window decorations. A
    floating dock widget without native decorations also draws
    \c PE_FrameDockWidget. The float and close buttons are tool buttons that
    draw \c PE_PanelButtonTool and show the \c SP_TitleBarNormalButton and
    \c SP_TitleBarCloseButton icons.

    \list
    \li \c PE_FrameDockWidget (\c PM_DockWidgetFrameWidth), when floating
    \li \c CE_DockWidgetTitle (\c SE_DockWidgetTitleBarText,
        \c SE_DockWidgetIcon, \c PM_DockWidgetTitleMargin)
    \li \c SE_DockWidgetFloatButton and \c SE_DockWidgetCloseButton
        (\c PM_DockWidgetTitleBarButtonMargin), the button positions
    \endlist

    \l QMainWindow draws the separators between docked widgets with
    \c PE_IndicatorDockWidgetResizeHandle, sized by
    \c PM_DockWidgetSeparatorExtent. The hint \c SH_DockWidget_ButtonsHaveFrame
    decides whether the title bar buttons have a frame.

    \image styles/dockwidget.webp
           {Main window with a dock widget on the right, with the title bar,
           title text, float and close buttons, and the separator outlined}

    The title bar option sets no state flags beyond the common ones. The members
    of \l QStyleOptionDockWidget are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li title
            \li The title text.
        \row
            \li closable
            \li Whether the dock widget can be closed.
        \row
            \li movable
            \li Whether the dock widget can be moved to another area.
        \row
            \li floatable
            \li Whether the dock widget can float, that is, detach from its main
                window.
        \row
            \li verticalTitleBar
            \li Whether the title bar is drawn vertically along the left side.
    \endtable

    \section1 Title bars

    The title bar complex control, \c CC_TitleBar, draws the title bars of the
    subwindows in a \l QMdiArea. It consists of a window title and the system
    menu, minimize, maximize, and close buttons. Some styles also provide
    buttons for shading the window and for context-sensitive help.

    \list
    \li \c CC_TitleBar (\c PM_TitleBarHeight, \c PM_TitleBarButtonSize,
        \c PM_TitleBarButtonIconSize)
        \list
        \li \c SC_TitleBarSysMenu
        \li \c SC_TitleBarLabel
        \li \c SC_TitleBarMinButton, \c SC_TitleBarMaxButton,
            \c SC_TitleBarCloseButton, \c SC_TitleBarNormalButton
        \li \c SC_TitleBarShadeButton, \c SC_TitleBarUnshadeButton,
            \c SC_TitleBarContextHelpButton
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \l QCommonStyle draws each button with \c PE_PanelButtonTool and the
    matching standard icon, from \c SP_TitleBarMenuButton to
    \c SP_TitleBarContextHelpButton. When a subwindow is maximized,
    \l QMdiSubWindow moves its buttons into the menu bar of the main window and
    draws them with \c CC_MdiControls and the subcontrols \c SC_MdiMinButton,
    \c SC_MdiNormalButton, and \c SC_MdiCloseButton.

    \image styles/titlebar.webp
           {Title bar with an icon, the text Sub window, and minimize, maximize,
           and close buttons, with each subcontrol outlined}

    The members of \l QStyleOptionTitleBar are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li text
            \li The title text.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The window icon.
        \row
            \li titleBarFlags
            \li \l{Qt::WindowFlags} that tell which buttons the title bar has.
        \row
            \li titleBarState
            \li The \l QWidget::windowState() of the window, which decides
                whether the maximize or the restore button is shown.
    \endtable

    \section1 Size grips

    \l QSizeGrip draws one control element, \c CE_SizeGrip, with a
    \l QStyleOptionSizeGrip, and calculates its size hint with \c CT_SizeGrip.
    \l QStatusBar shows a size grip in its corner by default.

    \image styles/sizegrip.webp
           {Status bar with the message Ready and the size grip in the corner
           outlined}

    The size grip sets no state flags beyond the common ones.
    \l QStyleOptionSizeGrip has one member of its own:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li corner
            \li A \l{Qt::Corner} value: the corner of the window the grip is in.
    \endtable

    \section1 Rubber bands

    \l QRubberBand draws one control element, \c CE_RubberBand, with a
    \l QStyleOptionRubberBand. The style can shape the band through the
    \c SH_RubberBand_Mask style hint.

    \image styles/rubberband.webp
           {List view with a translucent rubber band selection rectangle
           outlined}

    The band sets no state flags beyond the common ones. The members of
    \l QStyleOptionRubberBand are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li shape
            \li A \l QRubberBand::Shape value: a rectangle or a line.
        \row
            \li opaque
            \li Whether the band must be drawn opaquely.
    \endtable

    \sa {Widget Style Reference}, {Styling Buttons and Input Widgets},
        {Styling Menus and Item Views}
*/
